WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.524
Galaxy kinematics modeling is the computational
bottleneck when astronomers try to jointly

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:10.131
model gravitational lensing and kinematics to
measure the Hubble constant.

00:00:10.211 --> 00:00:14.303
This paper introduces SKiNN, the Stellar Kinematics
Neural Network,

00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:21.339
a proof-of-concept emulator that reproduces slow
physics-based kinematics calculations at a fraction of the cost.

00:00:21.419 --> 00:00:23.331
After a one-time training phase,

00:00:23.411 --> 00:00:28.811
SKiNN generates velocity dispersion images that are
accurate to within about one percent

00:00:28.891 --> 00:00:31.239
in the scientifically important region,

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:35.971
while running two to three orders of magnitude
faster than existing methods.

00:00:36.051 --> 00:00:42.696
This speedup finally makes it feasible to model
spatially resolved kinematic data jointly with lensing data,

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:47.490
addressing the largest source of uncertainty in
Hubble constant measurements.
